QUESTIONS.push(
/* ======================= ELIMINATION ======================= */
{id:'el-1', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'routes', concept:'elim-definition',
 tags:['definition'], source:'slide',
 stem:'How is elimination defined?',
 options:[
  {t:'Irreversible physical removal of metabolized and unmetabolized xenobiotics from the body', correct:true,
   why:'Irreversible is the word that does the work. Once a molecule is eliminated it cannot return to the central compartment, which is what separates elimination from every reversible process a drug undergoes. Both metabolized and unmetabolized forms count, so a drug that leaves unchanged in urine has been eliminated just as fully as one that left as a glucuronide.'},
  {t:'Chemical conversion of a drug into a more polar metabolite', correct:false,
   why:'That is metabolism, Lecture #10 (MCMet-9_4_26-9_9_26.pdf). Metabolism changes what the molecule is and usually raises its polarity so that a route can carry it out, but the molecule is still in the body afterwards. Metabolism prepares for elimination without being elimination.'},
  {t:'Reversible movement of drug out of plasma into tissue', correct:false,
   why:'That is distribution, from Dr. Yendapally\'s 090426_Distribution.pdf. It is reversible by definition: drug that moved into fat or muscle returns to plasma as the plasma concentration falls, which is why a highly distributed drug has a long terminal half-life without being cleared any faster.'},
  {t:'Binding of drug to plasma proteins to prevent activity', correct:false,
   why:'Protein binding is also a distribution phenomenon. It holds drug inside the plasma compartment rather than removing it, and because only unbound drug is filtered at the glomerulus, heavy binding slows elimination instead of accomplishing it.'}],
 svg:DIA.routeRank,
 teach:'Elimination, metabolism and distribution are three different events, and questions on this lecture usually turn on telling them apart. Distribution moves a drug between compartments and reverses. Metabolism changes the molecule chemically, most often making it more polar so a route can carry it. Elimination physically removes the molecule, and nothing that has been eliminated comes back. That last property is why elimination sets the drug half-life while distribution only delays it. The definition also covers unmetabolized drug, which matters because polar drugs such as atenolol, ampicillin and vancomycin leave in urine essentially unchanged and never need a Phase 1 or Phase 2 step at all.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 5, 8; distribution in 090426_Distribution.pdf; metabolism in Lecture #10 (MCMet-9_4_26-9_9_26.pdf)',
 quote:'Irreversible physical removal of metabolized & unmetabolized xenobiotics from the body'},

{id:'el-2', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'routes', concept:'route-ranking',
 tags:['ranking','reasoning'], source:'both',
 stem:'Rank the routes of elimination from major to minor.',
 options:[
  {t:'Renal, then hepatic/biliary/fecal, then intestinal and lung, then minor routes such as milk, sweat, hair and saliva', correct:true,
   why:'Renal is the primary route, because most drugs are made polar before they leave and polar unbound molecules filter at the glomerulus. Bile is the largest of the remaining routes and takes the lipid-soluble material that the kidney handles poorly. Intestinal and pulmonary elimination follow, each restricted to particular drugs. The remainder carry too little to affect clearance.'},
  {t:'Hepatic/biliary first, then renal, then lung', correct:false,
   why:'Bile does rank first among the non-renal routes, so this ordering is correct from the second position onward. It fails only at the top: renal handles the polar free or unbound drugs and metabolites, which is most of what reaches the elimination stage.'},
  {t:'Lung first, since exhalation removes drug continuously', correct:false,
   why:'Exhalation is the primary route for volatile or gaseous agents, slide 22, and for inhaled anesthetics it is close to the only route. It ranks first for that one class rather than in general, because a non-volatile drug cannot enter the gas phase at all.'},
  {t:'Sweat and saliva first, because they operate constantly', correct:false,
   why:'Both appear in this lecture as routes that are not major, slides 24 and 26. Their use is monitoring and testing rather than clearance: saliva detects exposure from 5 to 48 hours afterwards, and neither removes enough drug to change a dosing decision.'}],
 svg:DIA.routeRank,
 teach:'The ranking follows from polarity. Metabolism drives most drugs toward more polar products, and polar unbound molecules are exactly what the glomerulus filters, so the renal route receives the bulk of the traffic. What stays lipid-soluble is moved into bile by active transport instead, which makes the biliary route the main alternative rather than a minor one. Every remaining route is defined by a physical property of the molecule rather than by capacity: exhalation needs volatility, intestinal elimination needs the drug to be unabsorbed or effluxed back into the lumen, and milk, sweat, tears, hair and saliva simply receive whatever small fraction partitions there. Ranking by capacity and reading each minor route as a property test answers most questions in this section.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 6–26',
 quote:'Renal/Urinary Primary route for polar free or unbound drugs and metabolite(s)',
 source:'transcript'},

{id:'el-3', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'routes', concept:'polar-renal-lipophilic-biliary',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Hydrophilic and lipophilic xenobiotics are eliminated predominantly by which routes, respectively?',
 options:[
  {t:'Renal for hydrophilic, hepatic/biliary/fecal for lipophilic', correct:true,
   why:'Polar unbound drug filters at the glomerulus and, being charged or highly water soluble, cannot diffuse back across the tubule, so it stays in urine. Lipid-soluble drug would diffuse straight back, so the body moves it into bile by active transport instead and it leaves in feces.'},
  {t:'Hepatic for hydrophilic, renal for lipophilic', correct:false,
   why:'This is the correct pair of routes with the molecules swapped. The failure is on the renal side: a lipophilic drug does reach the tubule, but being un-ionized and lipid soluble it is reabsorbed by passive back diffusion, which is why phenytoin is named on slide 7 as subject to tubular reabsorption.'},
  {t:'Both by the renal route', correct:false,
   why:'True for the hydrophilic half only. The renal route is poor at removing lipophilic drug for the reabsorption reason above, which is the whole reason a second major route exists.'},
  {t:'Both by exhalation', correct:false,
   why:'Exhalation carries volatile or gaseous agents, slide 22, and volatility is unrelated to whether a molecule is water or lipid soluble. Inhaled anesthetics leave this way; neither a polar conjugate nor a heavy lipophilic drug can.'}],
 svg:DIA.routeRank,
 teach:'One property decides both halves of this pairing: whether the molecule can cross a membrane passively. Polar drug cannot, so once it is filtered into the tubule it is committed to urine. Lipophilic drug can, so filtering it accomplishes nothing because it returns to circulation from the tubule. The body solves that with active transport into bile, which does not depend on the molecule being polar. This also explains why Phase 2 conjugation helps elimination so much: attaching glucuronic acid or sulfate converts a molecule of the second kind into one of the first, and it stops being reabsorbable.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 7, 16',
 quote:'Hepatic/Bile/Fecal Primary route for lipid-soluble drugs, metabolites'},

{id:'el-4', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'renal', concept:'gfr-requirements',
 tags:['reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which two properties does a molecule need in order to be filtered at the glomerulus?',
 options:[
  {t:'Low molecular weight, below roughly 60 kDa, and being unbound to plasma protein', correct:true,
   why:'The glomerulus filters by size, so anything above about 60 kDa is held back. A drug bound to albumin travels as part of a complex far larger than that cutoff, so binding excludes it by the same size rule rather than by a separate mechanism.'},
  {t:'High molecular weight and high protein binding', correct:false,
   why:'These are the two conditions that prevent filtration, stated in reverse. Recognising them is still useful: a heavily bound drug such as warfarin depends on metabolism rather than filtration to be cleared.'},
  {t:'High lipophilicity and a neutral charge', correct:false,
   why:'Those two properties govern what happens after filtration, not whether it occurs. A small, un-ionized, lipophilic drug filters perfectly well and is then reabsorbed by passive back diffusion from the tubule, slide 12, so net elimination is low.'},
  {t:'Being a substrate for hepatic UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT)', correct:false,
   why:'Glucuronidation belongs to Phase 2 metabolism. It helps renal elimination indirectly by making the molecule polar enough to resist reabsorption, but the glomerulus applies no such test: an unconjugated drug that is small and unbound is filtered just the same.'}],
 svg:DIA.renalHandling,
 teach:'Filtration is a size filter applied to whatever is free in plasma, and the two requirements are really one requirement counted twice. The pore cutoff of roughly 60 kDa excludes large molecules, and albumin binding excludes drug by attaching it to a molecule that is itself too large. Nothing about charge, lipophilicity or metabolic history is tested at this step. Those properties matter immediately afterwards, in the tubule, where the un-ionized form diffuses back into circulation and the ionized form stays and is excreted. Separating the two stages, filtration by size and reabsorption by charge, keeps the renal questions in this lecture straight, because inflammation raising the glomerular filtration rate acts on the first stage while urinary pH acts on the second.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 11',
 quote:'Low MW (<60 kDa) are filtered · Unbound or free drugs are filtered'},

{id:'el-5', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'renal', concept:'tubular-reabsorption-ionization',
 tags:['reasoning','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'In the renal tubule, which species is reabsorbed and by what mechanism?',
 options:[
  {t:'The un-ionized drug, mostly by passive back diffusion', correct:true,
   why:'An un-ionized molecule carries no charge, so it dissolves in the lipid of the tubular membrane and crosses back into circulation without a transporter. The ionized form cannot cross, so it remains in the tubular fluid and is excreted.'},
  {t:'The ionized drug, by active transport', correct:false,
   why:'Both halves belong elsewhere. The ionized drug is the species excreted, not reabsorbed, and active transport is the mechanism of tubular secretion, slide 13, which moves drug from blood into the tubule rather than back out of it.'},
  {t:'Protein-bound drug, by endocytosis', correct:false,
   why:'Protein-bound drug never reaches the tubule, because the albumin complex is too large to be filtered. It is excluded one step earlier, at the glomerulus.'},
  {t:'Both forms equally, by bulk flow with water', correct:false,
   why:'If both forms behaved alike, urinary pH could not change elimination at all. The difference between the two forms is precisely what pH trapping exploits, slide 14.'}],
 svg:DIA.renalHandling,
 teach:'Reabsorption is passive, so it selects on one property only: whether the molecule can dissolve in membrane lipid. Charge prevents that, which puts every ionizable drug under the control of the surrounding pH. A weak acid in acidic urine is mostly un-ionized and returns to circulation; the same acid in alkaline urine is mostly ionized and leaves. Because the mechanism is diffusion rather than transport, it also runs down a concentration gradient, so as water is reabsorbed and tubular drug concentration rises the gradient favouring reabsorption strengthens. Tubular secretion is the opposite arrangement in both respects, being active and moving drug into the tubule, which is why probenecid can slow penicillin excretion by blocking it.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 12',
 quote:'The un-ionized drug is reabsorbed from the renal tubule back into circulation (mostly via passive back diffusion)'},

{id:'el-6', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'renal', concept:'probenecid-penicillin',
 tags:['case','transporter'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Probenecid slows the excretion of penicillin. What is the mechanism?',
 options:[
  {t:'It inhibits renal organic anion transporters, reducing tubular secretion of penicillin', correct:true,
   why:'Penicillin carries a carboxylic acid, so at urine and plasma pH it travels as an anion and leaves largely by active secretion rather than by filtration alone. Anion secretion uses organic anion transporting polypeptides 1 and 3 and multidrug resistance associated proteins 2 and 4, and probenecid competes at those carriers, so penicillin stays in circulation longer.'},
  {t:'It inhibits CYP3A4, slowing hepatic metabolism of penicillin', correct:false,
   why:'This describes a genuine class of interaction, the kind covered throughout MCMet-9_15_2026.pdf, but applied to the wrong drug and the wrong organ. Penicillin is cleared renally as intact drug, so its clearance does not depend on a CYP.'},
  {t:'It alkalinizes the urine and traps penicillin there', correct:false,
   why:'Alkalinizing urine would ionize an acidic drug such as penicillin and prevent its reabsorption, which speeds elimination. That is the aspirin overdose intervention on slide 15, and it produces the opposite of the effect described here.'},
  {t:'It displaces penicillin from plasma protein binding sites', correct:false,
   why:'Displacement raises the unbound fraction, and unbound drug is the fraction that filters. That would increase elimination rather than slow it, so it belongs among distribution interactions rather than as an explanation for a longer penicillin half-life.'}],
 svg:DIA.renalHandling,
 teach:'Tubular secretion is the third renal process, alongside filtration and reabsorption, and it is the only one that is saturable and competitive, because it runs on carriers. Anions move through organic anion transporting polypeptides 1 and 3 and multidrug resistance associated proteins 2 and 4; cations move through organic cation transporter 2, multidrug resistance protein 1, and the multidrug and toxin extrusion carriers MATE1 and MATE2K. Two drugs handled by the same carrier compete, and the effect is deliberately exploited here: slowing penicillin excretion raises and prolongs its concentration. Transporter interactions are recognised by the pattern of a renally cleared drug whose clearance changes with no metabolic explanation available.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 13',
 quote:'Probenecid (inhibits renal OATPs & slows Penicillin excretion)'},

{id:'el-7', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'ph', concept:'ph-trapping-definition',
 tags:['definition','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What is pH trapping?',
 options:[
  {t:'A drug that ionizes in urine cannot be reabsorbed, so it is trapped in the tubule and eliminated', correct:true,
   why:'Ionization removes the molecule\'s ability to cross the tubular membrane, so once it has been filtered and then charged by the surrounding pH, the only path available is onward into urine.'},
  {t:'A drug binds to tubular protein and is held in the kidney', correct:false,
   why:'Retention by binding happens in other settings, but nothing binds the drug here. The molecule is free in the tubular fluid; what holds it there is its own charge, which is why the effect can be switched on and off by changing urine pH.'},
  {t:'Urine pH changes so the drug precipitates and is filtered out', correct:false,
   why:'Drug precipitating in the tubule is a nephrotoxicity mechanism rather than a clearance mechanism, and it damages the kidney. pH trapping keeps the drug dissolved and simply prevents its return across the membrane.'},
  {t:'The drug is recycled between bile and intestine before elimination', correct:false,
   why:'That is enterohepatic recycling, slide 18, which is hepatic rather than renal and delays elimination instead of promoting it.'}],
 svg:DIA.renalHandling,
 teach:'pH trapping is tubular reabsorption run deliberately in reverse. Reabsorption requires an un-ionized molecule, so shifting the tubular pH away from the drug\'s own character converts it to the charged form and strips away its ability to return. The requirement is a functional group that can gain or lose a proton, and it is a real condition rather than a detail: a drug with no ionizable group is unaffected by urine pH no matter how far the pH is moved. The direction follows the character of the drug, so an acid is trapped by alkaline urine and a base by acidic urine, and the two directions are stated separately on slide 14.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 14',
 quote:'a drug ionizes in urine & cannot be reabsorbed - it is "trapped" & eliminated'},

{id:'el-8', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'ph', concept:'ph-trapping-requirement',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'both',
 stem:'What must a drug possess for pH trapping to work on it?',
 options:[
  {t:'An ionizable functional group, such as a carboxylic acid or an amine', correct:true,
   why:'Changing urine pH changes the ratio of ionized to un-ionized drug, and a molecule with no proton to gain or lose has no such ratio to shift. A carboxylic acid loses a proton as pH rises and an amine gains one as pH falls, so each responds to a move in one direction.'},
  {t:'High lipophilicity, with log P above 3', correct:false,
   why:'High lipophilicity promotes exactly what trapping is meant to prevent. A lipophilic un-ionized molecule crosses the tubular membrane readily and returns to circulation, which is why phenytoin is named on slide 7 as subject to tubular reabsorption.'},
  {t:'A molecular weight above 60 kDa', correct:false,
   why:'That is above the glomerular cutoff on slide 11, so such a molecule is never filtered and never enters the tubule. It is excluded one stage before trapping could apply.'},
  {t:'Extensive plasma protein binding', correct:false,
   why:'Bound drug is not filtered either, for the same size reason, so it also fails to reach the tubule. Protein binding governs how much drug is available to the kidney, not what happens to it once there.'}],
 svg:DIA.renalHandling,
 teach:'This condition is what limits the technique in practice. Urine pH is a lever that acts on the acid-base equilibrium of the drug, so the drug must have such an equilibrium for the lever to connect to anything. Neutral drugs with no acidic or basic group are unaffected however far urine pH is moved. The condition also explains why the useful cases are the ones named in this lecture: aspirin is a carboxylic acid, so alkalinizing urine ionizes it and traps it. Two further conditions are implied by the stages before trapping, since the drug must be small enough to filter and unbound enough to be presented to the glomerulus at all.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 14',
 quote:'Key: Drugs/metabolites must have ionizable FGs'},

{id:'el-9', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'ph', concept:'aspirin-overdose-alkalinize',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Aspirin overdose is treated by alkalinizing the urine with sodium bicarbonate. Why does that help?',
 options:[
  {t:'Aspirin is acidic, so basic urine ionizes it, preventing reabsorption and forcing elimination', correct:true,
   why:'Raising urine pH above the drug\'s pKa converts salicylate to its charged carboxylate form. The charged form cannot diffuse back across the tubular membrane, so it stays in the tubular fluid and leaves in urine.'},
  {t:'Aspirin is basic, so basic urine keeps it un-ionized and easier to excrete', correct:false,
   why:'Two errors compound here. Aspirin carries a carboxylic acid rather than a basic group, and the un-ionized form is the one that gets reabsorbed. This answer would be the right shape for a basic drug in acidic urine, which is the situation in the matching question on the other direction.'},
  {t:'Bicarbonate chemically destroys aspirin in the bladder', correct:false,
   why:'No bond in the molecule is broken. Bicarbonate acts only as a base that raises urine pH, and the drug leaves chemically intact; the intervention shifts an equilibrium rather than running a reaction.'},
  {t:'Bicarbonate induces the enzymes that metabolize aspirin', correct:false,
   why:'Induction takes days to develop, which is far too slow for an overdose, and it is a metabolic intervention covered in MCMet-9_15_2026.pdf. This treatment acts on renal handling of drug already in the tubule and works within the current dosing interval.'}],
 svg:DIA.renalHandling,
 teach:'This is pH trapping used as therapy, and it shows why the direction has to be reasoned rather than recalled. Aspirin is an acid, so it is ionized by a rise in pH; alkalinizing the urine therefore maximises the charged fraction in the tubule and blocks its return. The same logic inverts for a basic drug, which is ionized by a fall in pH and so is trapped by acidifying the urine. Enhanced elimination of this kind appears again among the toxicity treatments on slide 62, alongside forced diuresis and dialysis, and the requirement from slide 14 still applies: without an ionizable group there is nothing for the pH change to act on.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 14–15',
 quote:'Aspirin overdose is treated by alkalizing urine with NaHCO3'},

{id:'el-10', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'ph', concept:'ph-trapping-direction-base',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'both',
 stem:'A patient has overdosed on a basic drug. How would you manipulate urine pH, and why?',
 options:[
  {t:'Acidify the urine, so the basic drug is protonated, trapped and eliminated', correct:true,
   why:'A base gains a proton in acidic conditions and becomes a cation. The charged form cannot diffuse back across the tubular membrane, so it remains in the tubular fluid and is excreted.'},
  {t:'Alkalinize the urine, so the basic drug stays un-ionized and leaves faster', correct:false,
   why:'The reasoning inverts the key fact: the un-ionized form is the reabsorbable one, so alkaline urine keeps a basic drug neutral and returns it to circulation. This answer would be correct for an acidic drug, which is the aspirin case on slide 15.'},
  {t:'Leave urine pH alone, since pH affects only acidic drugs', correct:false,
   why:'Both directions are set out on slide 14: acidic urine decreases elimination of acidic drugs and increases elimination of basic drugs, while basic urine increases elimination of acidic drugs and decreases elimination of basic ones. Each pH helps one class and hinders the other, so pH manipulation is available for either. Only a drug with no ionizable group is exempt, because it cannot be trapped in a charged form at any pH.'},
  {t:'Alkalinize the urine to precipitate the drug in the tubule', correct:false,
   why:'Deliberately precipitating drug in the tubule would damage the kidney. Crystal formation belongs with the nephrotoxicity mechanisms in the toxicity half of this lecture rather than among treatments.'}],
 svg:DIA.renalHandling,
 teach:'One rule covers both directions: a drug leaves when it is charged in urine, so the urine is moved to the pH opposite the drug\'s own character. An acid is charged in alkaline urine and a base is charged in acidic urine. Working from the molecule rather than from a remembered pairing also handles the cases where the drug carries both an acidic and a basic group, and it makes the reverse question tractable, since a patient whose urine has drifted the wrong way will clear an ionizable drug more slowly. The requirement from slide 14 still gates the whole approach, because a drug with no ionizable functional group responds to none of this.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 14',
 quote:'Acidic urine: ↓ elim. of acidic drugs & ↑ elim. of basic drugs',
 source:'transcript',
 note:'One sentence covers both directions: the drug leaves when it is charged in urine, so match the urine pH to the opposite character of the drug.'},

{id:'el-11', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'hep', concept:'enterohepatic-definition',
 tags:['definition','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What is enterohepatic recycling?',
 options:[
  {t:'Drug excreted in bile into the intestine is reabsorbed back into circulation before it can be eliminated in feces', correct:true,
   why:'The loop runs liver to bile to intestine and back to circulation. Each pass returns drug that had already been committed to elimination, so the effect is to delay clearance and extend duration of action rather than to change the amount eventually removed.'},
  {t:'Drug is filtered at the glomerulus then reabsorbed from the renal tubule', correct:false,
   why:'That is tubular reabsorption, slide 12. The shape is similar, in that both return drug that was on its way out, but the organ, the mechanism and the intervention all differ: reabsorption in the tubule is passive and is manipulated with urine pH.'},
  {t:'Drug moves repeatedly between plasma and tissue stores', correct:false,
   why:'That is distribution and redistribution. It changes where drug sits in the body and can prolong a terminal half-life, but no part of it involves an elimination route, so nothing was ever excreted and returned.'},
  {t:'Drug is metabolized then re-metabolized by the same enzyme', correct:false,
   why:'Sequential metabolism does occur, most commonly Phase 1 followed by Phase 2, but recycling here names physical movement of the molecule through bile and gut. The enzymatic step in the loop is performed by gut bacteria, not by the hepatic enzyme that conjugated the drug.'}],
 svg:DIA.enterohepatic,
 teach:'Recycling is what makes the biliary route leaky. Drug delivered into the intestine has two fates, leaving in feces or crossing the gut wall again, and each return trip resets the clock on its elimination. The clinical signature is a long duration of action out of proportion to the drug\'s metabolic clearance, which is why ezetimibe is the lecture\'s example: repeated cycling gives it a half-life of 22 to 24 hours. Isotretinoin and morphine recycle as well. The loop is also the reason oral activated charcoal and anion exchange resins help in overdose, since binding drug in the lumen removes the reabsorption option and forces the fecal route.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 18–19',
 quote:'Drugs excreted via bile into the intestine undergo reabsorption ("recycling") prior to fecal elim.'},

{id:'el-12', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'hep', concept:'enterohepatic-glucuronidase',
 tags:['mechanism','reasoning'], source:'both',
 stem:'A drug is glucuronidated and excreted in bile, yet parent drug reappears in circulation. What happened?',
 options:[
  {t:'Intestinal bacterial glucuronidases cleaved the glucuronide, regenerating the parent drug for reabsorption', correct:true,
   why:'The conjugate is polar, which is what allowed biliary transport and would otherwise have carried it out in feces. Gut bacteria hydrolyse the glucuronide bond and release the original lipophilic drug, which can then cross the intestinal wall by passive diffusion.'},
  {t:'The glucuronide itself was reabsorbed unchanged and hydrolyzed in plasma', correct:false,
   why:'The order is wrong, and the order is the mechanism. A polar conjugate crosses the intestinal wall poorly, so cleavage has to precede reabsorption; it is the loss of the sugar that restores the lipophilicity needed to be absorbed.'},
  {t:'Renal tubular secretion returned the drug to blood', correct:false,
   why:'Tubular secretion moves drug from blood into the tubule, so it works in the opposite direction, and it belongs to the renal route on slide 13 rather than the biliary one described here.'},
  {t:'The drug was re-synthesized by hepatic UDP-glucuronosyltransferase running in reverse', correct:false,
   why:'UDP-glucuronosyltransferase attaches glucuronic acid and does not remove it. The deconjugating enzyme is bacterial and sits in the intestinal lumen, which is why the step happens after biliary excretion rather than in the liver.'}],
 svg:DIA.enterohepatic,
 teach:'This is the chemical detail underneath enterohepatic recycling, and it explains why conjugation does not always finish the job. Phase 2 makes a drug polar so a route can carry it, and biliary transport accepts that polar conjugate readily. In the gut, bacterial glucuronidases reverse the conjugation, and the drug is restored to the lipophilic form that crossed membranes in the first place. The consequence is that Phase 2 metabolism guarantees elimination only if the conjugate survives the intestine. It also explains why altering gut flora, with antibiotics for example, can change the exposure of a recycling drug without any change to hepatic metabolism.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 18–20; lecture audio 09/17 (marked)',
 quote:'*Zetia undergoes repeated cycling - long DoA (t1/2= 22-24 h)',
 note:'Slide 19 names the recycling examples as ezetimibe (Zetia), isotretinoin and morphine.'},

{id:'el-13', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'hep', concept:'charcoal-interrupts-recycling',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'How can enterohepatic recycling be interrupted clinically?',
 options:[
  {t:'Oral activated charcoal or anion exchange resins, which trap the drug in the gut for fecal elimination', correct:true,
   why:'Both bind drug inside the intestinal lumen, so it is no longer free to cross the gut wall. Removing the reabsorption option breaks the loop and commits the drug to the fecal route.'},
  {t:'Alkalinizing the urine with sodium bicarbonate', correct:false,
   why:'That is the renal pH trapping intervention from slide 15, used for an acidic drug such as aspirin. It acts in the tubule and has no effect on drug circulating between bile and intestine.'},
  {t:'Inducing CYP3A4 with rifampin', correct:false,
   why:'Induction increases metabolic capacity over days, from MCMet-9_15_2026.pdf. Recycling is a transport and absorption phenomenon, so making the liver metabolize faster does not stop conjugated drug being reabsorbed from the gut.'},
  {t:'Giving probenecid to block tubular secretion', correct:false,
   why:'Probenecid slows renal elimination by competing at organic anion transporters, slide 13. In an overdose that is the wrong direction entirely, since it retains drug rather than removing it.'}],
 svg:DIA.enterohepatic,
 teach:'The intervention follows directly from where the loop can be broken. Drug in the intestinal lumen is the one point in the cycle that an orally administered agent can reach, and binding it there converts a reabsorbable molecule into one committed to feces. Activated charcoal adsorbs a wide range of drugs and anion exchange resins bind anionic ones, and both act without being absorbed themselves. This sits alongside the other enhanced-elimination measures listed on slide 62, forced diuresis, ion trapping, dialysis and hemoperfusion, each of which targets a different compartment; the choice follows from where the drug actually is.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 18',
 quote:'Can be clinically disrupted using PO charcoal or anion exchange resins to trap drugs in the GI tract for fecal elim.'},

{id:'el-14', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'routes', concept:'pulmonary-elimination',
 tags:['recall','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which drugs leave primarily by exhalation, and by what mechanism?',
 options:[
  {t:'Volatile or gaseous agents such as inhaled anesthetics, by passive diffusion', correct:true,
   why:'A volatile molecule can enter the gas phase, so it crosses the alveolar membrane down its concentration gradient and is carried out in expired air. Inhaled anesthetics leave largely this way and largely unchanged, so their offset depends on ventilation rather than on metabolism.'},
  {t:'Large polar conjugates, by active transport', correct:false,
   why:'Polar conjugates are renally eliminated, and their water solubility is exactly what prevents volatilisation. Active transport is also the wrong mechanism for this route, since the lung route is passive.'},
  {t:'Protein-bound lipophilic drugs, by filtration', correct:false,
   why:'Filtration is the glomerular mechanism, and protein-bound drug is not filtered even there because the complex exceeds the size cutoff on slide 11.'},
  {t:'Any drug with a carboxylic acid group, by ion trapping', correct:false,
   why:'Ion trapping is a urinary phenomenon from slide 14, and a charged carboxylate cannot enter the gas phase at all. An ionizable group makes a drug a candidate for pH manipulation in the kidney, not for exhalation.'}],
 svg:DIA.routeRank,
 teach:'Each minor route is defined by a physical property, and for the lung that property is volatility. A molecule must be able to leave solution for the gas phase, which restricts the route to a small set of agents and makes it close to the only route for those. Because the mechanism is passive diffusion down a gradient, increasing ventilation increases elimination, which is how anesthesia is reversed at the end of a case. The same reasoning identifies the other routes: the biliary route needs lipid solubility with active transport, the renal route needs polarity, and the intestinal route needs the drug to be unabsorbed or effluxed back into the lumen.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 22',
 quote:'Lungs/Exhalation- Primary route for volatile or gaseous agents'},

{id:'el-15', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'routes', concept:'sweat-both-logp',
 tags:['reasoning'], source:'transcript',
 stem:'True or false: both high log P and low log P drugs can be eliminated in sweat.',
 options:[
  {t:'True — skin secretes both watery and oily material, so lipophilic and hydrophilic molecules can both appear', correct:true,
   why:'The skin route covers sweat and oil together, and the two secretions differ in character. A water-soluble drug partitions into the aqueous sweat and a lipid-soluble one into sebum, so polarity does not exclude a drug from this route the way it does from the renal or pulmonary routes.'},
  {t:'False — only hydrophilic drugs appear in sweat because sweat is water', correct:false,
   why:'This holds for the aqueous secretion alone and overlooks the oily one. Sebaceous output gives lipophilic molecules a route of their own, which is why the slide heading pairs sweat with oil.'},
  {t:'False — only lipophilic drugs appear, because they cross skin lipid', correct:false,
   why:'This is the same error in reverse, taking the oily half as the whole. Crossing skin lipid describes transdermal absorption inward; elimination outward uses both secretions.'},
  {t:'True — but only for drugs given transdermally', correct:false,
   why:'The conclusion is right but the restriction is invented. What appears in sweat depends on the drug partitioning there from the circulation, so how the drug entered the body does not determine whether it can leave by this route.'}],
 svg:DIA.routeRank,
 teach:'The skin route differs from the major routes in not selecting on polarity, because the skin puts out two secretions of opposite character. That makes it unusual among the routes in this lecture, nearly all of which are defined by a single physical property the molecule must have. What limits the skin route is capacity rather than selectivity: very little drug leaves this way, so its use is monitoring and testing rather than clearance. Hair and saliva are limited in the same manner, with saliva detecting exposure from 5 to 48 hours afterwards, and the tears route is noted mainly because rifampin gives an orange-red coloration that stains contact lenses.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 24',
 quote:'Skin/Sweat/Oil - Not a maj. route but may be used to monitor/test'},

{id:'el-16', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'elim', sub:'routes', concept:'minor-routes-monitoring',
 tags:['reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Hair and saliva are minor routes of elimination. What is their practical value?',
 options:[
  {t:'Monitoring and testing for drug exposure rather than clearance', correct:true,
   why:'Neither route removes enough drug to affect a dosing decision, but the small amount that does appear can be measured. Hair grows slowly and records exposure over a long window, while a saliva swab detects recent exposure from 5 to 48 hours afterwards, so the two answer different questions about when a drug was taken.'},
  {t:'They are the main route for polar metabolites', correct:false,
   why:'Polar metabolites leave renally, since polar free or unbound drug and metabolites are what the kidney handles, slide 7.'},
  {t:'They eliminate volatile agents', correct:false,
   why:'Volatile and gaseous agents leave by exhalation through passive diffusion across the alveolar membrane, slide 22.'},
  {t:'They are the main route in renal failure', correct:false,
   why:'When renal function falls the burden shifts to the biliary and intestinal routes, which have real capacity. Minor routes do not expand to compensate, which is why dose adjustment is required in kidney disease, slide 10.'}],
 svg:DIA.routeRank,
 teach:'A route can be useful without being quantitatively important, and this lecture lists several of that kind. Hair, saliva, sweat and tears carry too little drug to alter clearance, so their value is the information they hold: saliva for recent exposure, hair for a long retrospective window. Breast milk is the exception among the minor routes, because the amount that matters is not the fraction of the dose but the dose delivered to a nursing infant, with topotecan and cimetidine named on slide 23. Tears are noted for rifampin, which colours them orange-red and can permanently stain contact lenses.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 23–26',
 quote:'Saliva (mouth swabs)- Not a maj. route of elimin. - may be used to monitor/test'},

/* ======================= TOXICITY ======================= */
{id:'tx-1', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'causes', concept:'toxicophore-definition',
 tags:['definition'], source:'both',
 stem:'What is a toxicophore?',
 options:[
  {t:'The smallest structural unit of a molecule that can lead to toxicity', correct:true,
   why:'The term is built to parallel pharmacophore, which names the smallest unit carrying pharmacological activity. In practice a toxicophore is a functional group with reactive electronic character: an epoxide, an aldehyde, a halogenated carbon, a conjugated double bond.'},
  {t:'The smallest unit of a molecule responsible for its therapeutic activity', correct:false,
   why:'That is the pharmacophore, and the two terms are deliberately built on the same pattern. One molecule commonly carries both, which is why a drug can be effective and hazardous for structural reasons that sit side by side.'},
  {t:'A metabolite that has lost all biological activity', correct:false,
   why:'An inactive metabolite is the safe endpoint of metabolism, and the conjugates formed in Phase 2 are the usual examples. A toxicophore is defined by being reactive rather than inert.'},
  {t:'A protein site that binds drugs covalently', correct:false,
   why:'That is the nucleophilic target the toxicophore attacks, not the toxicophore itself. The two halves of an adduct are easy to swap: the electrophilic group belongs to the drug and the electron-rich site belongs to the protein or DNA.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teachImg:'tx_halothane',
 teach:'A toxicophore is a structural feature rather than a whole molecule, which is why toxicity can often be predicted from a drawing. The lecture divides them by electronic character: five bullets on slide 30: electrophilic carbons, electrophilic carbonyls, electrophilic double bonds, free radicals, and a fifth group of others covering haptens, azo compounds and N-hydroxy groups. The first three share one logic, an electron-poor centre waiting for an electron-rich one. A toxicophore may be present in the parent drug already, as with a halogenated or allylic carbon, or it may be created by metabolism, as with an epoxide. That split into existing and bioactivated runs through the whole toxicity section, and it decides whether avoiding harm means changing the molecule or changing the metabolism.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 29–30',
 quote:'Toxicophores (toxic/potentially toxic FGs)'},

{id:'tx-2', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'causes', concept:'causes-of-toxicity-list',
 tags:['recall'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which of the following is a cause of on-target or off-target drug toxicity?',
 options:[
  {t:'Low deactivating enzyme capacity from genetics or inhibitors', correct:true,
   why:'Deactivating capacity decides how much reactive material survives long enough to do harm. A poor metabolizer phenotype or a co-administered inhibitor lowers that capacity, so a dose that is safe in most patients is not safe in this one.'},
  {t:'High plasma albumin concentration', correct:false,
   why:'Binding holds drug in plasma and lowers the unbound fraction available to tissues, so it generally moves risk downward. The distribution-related entry on the causes list refers to interactions that displace drug from binding, which raises free concentration rather than lowering it.'},
  {t:'Rapid glomerular filtration', correct:false,
   why:'Efficient filtration speeds elimination and works against accumulation. The opposite case, kidney disease reducing clearance, is what requires dose adjustment, slide 10.'},
  {t:'Extensive Phase 2 conjugation', correct:false,
   why:'Conjugation is the protective arm and normally ends reactivity. It enters the toxicity story only when it runs out: acetaminophen is safe while sulfation and glucuronidation keep up, and becomes hepatotoxic once they are exhausted, slide 49.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teach:'Six causes are listed, and they divide into properties of the molecule and properties of the patient. The molecule contributes toxicophores and its therapeutic index, since a narrow gap between effective and toxic concentrations leaves no room for ordinary variation. The patient contributes deactivating capacity, which genetics and inhibitors both lower, and the immune responses behind hypersensitivity and idiosyncratic reactions. Drug interactions sit between the two, changing the exposure a given dose produces. Reading a toxicity case usually means asking which of these is operating, because the intervention differs: a dose problem is solved by dosing, a capacity problem by checking interactions and phenotype, and an immune problem by stopping the drug.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 29',
 quote:'Low deactivating Enzyme capacity (genetics, inhibitors, etc.)'},

{id:'tx-3', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'toxicophore-categories',
 tags:['recall','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which set matches the toxicophore categories given in lecture?',
 options:[
  {t:'Electrophilic carbons, electrophilic carbonyls, electrophilic double bonds, free radicals, and others including haptens, azo and N-hydroxy groups', correct:true,
   why:'The first three are one idea applied at three different sites: an electron-poor centre open to attack by a nucleophile on protein or DNA. Free radicals work differently, by propagating rather than by waiting to be attacked, and the fifth bullet, others, collects mechanisms that are neither.'},
  {t:'Alcohols, ethers, alkanes and amides', correct:false,
   why:'These are ordinary metabolic substrates from MCMet-9_16_2026.pdf, handled routinely by Phase 1 and Phase 2 enzymes. None carries the electron-poor centre that defines a toxicophore.'},
  {t:'Glucuronides, sulfates and glutathione conjugates', correct:false,
   why:'These are the products of successful detoxification. A glutathione conjugate in particular is what a reactive electrophile becomes after a glutathione S-transferase has dealt with it, so it marks the end of the danger rather than its source.'},
  {t:'UDP-glucuronosyltransferase, sulfotransferase, N-acetyltransferase and glutathione S-transferase', correct:false,
   why:'These are the Phase 2 enzymes, not structural features of a molecule. A toxicophore is part of the drug; these act on it.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teachImg:'tx_dblbond',
 teach:'Sorting the categories by what makes each dangerous makes the examples predictable. An electrophilic carbon is electron poor because something nearby withdraws density: a halogen on the same carbon, slide 31, or an adjacent double bond in the allylic case, slide 32, or the strain of a three-membered epoxide ring, slide 33. An electrophilic carbonyl is electron poor at the carbon for the same reason, with aldehydes as the example on slide 36. An electrophilic double bond is created by conjugation, which spreads density and leaves particular carbons exposed. Free radicals need no partner at all and propagate on their own. The others group, haptens, azo compounds and N-hydroxy groups, is where the immune and carcinogenic mechanisms sit.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 30',
 quote:'Electrophilic carbons · Electrophilic carbonyls · Electrophilic double bonds · Free radicals (ROS/RNS included) · Others: Haptens, Azo, N-OH, etc.'},

{id:'tx-4', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'electrophile-nucleophile-adduct',
 tags:['mechanism','reasoning'], source:'both',
 stem:'Why does an electrophilic centre on a drug metabolite cause cellular damage?',
 options:[
  {t:'Nucleophilic sites on proteins and DNA attack it, forming a covalent adduct that disrupts normal function', correct:true,
   why:'Proteins and DNA are full of electron-rich sites, including cysteine thiols, lysine amines and the nitrogens of DNA bases. An electron-poor carbon draws one of these in, and the bond that forms is covalent, so the change is permanent: the protein no longer folds or functions as it did, and a modified DNA base can be miscopied.'},
  {t:'It chelates iron and depletes hemoglobin', correct:false,
   why:'Iron does appear in this lecture, but in the doxorubicin cardiotoxicity mechanism on slide 52, where mitochondrial iron helps generate reactive oxygen species. That is a radical mechanism rather than adduct formation, and it does not work by chelation.'},
  {t:'It raises intracellular pH and denatures enzymes', correct:false,
   why:'Denaturation by a pH shift is a bulk physical effect and would damage every protein at once. What is described here is specific covalent modification at particular nucleophilic sites, which is why toxicity from adducts targets particular tissues and particular proteins.'},
  {t:'It competes with ATP at kinase active sites', correct:false,
   why:'That is reversible competitive binding, the mechanism of a pharmacological agent rather than a toxicophore. The defining feature here is that the bond does not come undone.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teachImg:'tx_halothane',
 teach:'The whole electrophilic half of this lecture reduces to one pairing: an electron-poor centre on the drug and an electron-rich centre on a macromolecule. Because the bond formed is covalent, the damage accumulates rather than reversing when the drug is cleared, which is why a short exposure can produce a lasting injury. Where the adduct forms decides what is seen: protein adducts disable enzymes and can also create haptens that provoke an immune response, while DNA adducts introduce the mutations behind carcinogenesis, which is the route for aflatoxin B1 on slide 34 and benzo[a]pyrene on slide 35. The defenses on slide 60 work by getting there first, with glutathione S-transferases offering a nucleophile the electrophile can react with harmlessly.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 30–31',
 quote:'Existing or Bioactivated Toxicophores in parent drug, intermediates or metabolites'},

{id:'tx-5', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'allylic-carbon',
 tags:['structure','mechanism'], source:'both',
 stem:'An allylic carbon is flagged as an existing electrophilic toxicophore. What makes it electron deficient?',
 options:[
  {t:'The adjacent double bond pulls electron density away from that carbon', correct:true,
   why:'The allylic position is the saturated carbon next to a carbon-carbon double bond. Electron density shifts toward the pi system, leaving that carbon electron poor and open to nucleophilic attack without any metabolic step being required first.'},
  {t:'It carries a halogen substituent', correct:false,
   why:'That is the other existing electrophilic carbon, slide 31. A halogen is strongly electron withdrawing and leaves the carbon it sits on electron poor, so the outcome matches but the structural cause differs; halothane is the example there.'},
  {t:'It is part of a three-membered strained ring', correct:false,
   why:'That is an epoxide carbon, slide 33, which is a bioactivated rather than an existing electrophile because a CYP has to create the ring first. Ring strain rather than an adjacent group is what makes it reactive.'},
  {t:'It bears a free radical', correct:false,
   why:'Free radicals are a separate category on slide 30, with isoniazid and phenytoin as the examples. A radical carries an unpaired electron and propagates; an electrophilic carbon simply waits to be attacked.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teachImg:'tx_allylic_tamoxifen',
 teach:'Existing toxicophores are the ones present in the drug as given, so they matter at the design stage rather than depending on how a particular patient metabolizes the drug. Two are named: the halogenated carbon, where an electronegative halogen withdraws density directly, and the allylic carbon, where an adjacent double bond does the same through the pi system. Recognising them on a structure is a matter of looking for what sits next to the carbon in question. The contrast is with bioactivated toxicophores such as epoxides, which are absent until a CYP creates them, and that difference decides the intervention: an existing toxicophore is avoided by changing the molecule, a bioactivated one by considering the enzymes involved.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 31–32',
 quote:'Existing electrophilic carbons · Example: Allylic Carbons'},

{id:'tx-6', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'carbamazepine-epoxide',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Carbamazepine is oxidised by CYP3A4 to its 10,11-epoxide, and a protein nucleophile attacks that epoxide. What does the adduct look like, and what does that say about the epoxide?',
 options:[
  {t:'The ring opens: the nucleophile bonds to one epoxide carbon and the other carbon keeps the oxygen as a hydroxyl; the epoxide carbon was the electrophile', correct:true,
   why:'The general scheme at the top of slide 33 draws it: alkene, CYP450, epoxide, then the protein nucleophile attacks a ring carbon and the product is HO-C-C-Nuc-protein. The three-membered ring is strained and the carbons are electron-poor because of the oxygen, so a nucleophile opens it. The carbamazepine row below is the same reaction on a drug.'},
  {t:'The nucleophile adds across the alkene that the epoxide reverts to', correct:false,
   why:'The epoxide does not revert to the alkene; the oxygen stays in the product as a hydroxyl. An isolated alkene is not electrophilic enough for a protein nucleophile to attack, which is why the parent carbamazepine is not the toxic species and the epoxide is.'},
  {t:'The nucleophile attacks the carbamoyl carbonyl and the epoxide stays intact', correct:false,
   why:'The urea-type carbonyl on the ring nitrogen is a poor electrophile: both attached heteroatoms feed electron density into it. Slide 33 marks the epoxide in red as the reactive site, and the carbonyl is untouched in the adduct.'},
  {t:'The epoxide oxygen is transferred to the protein as a hydroxyl radical', correct:false,
   why:'No radical is involved. The epoxide is an electrophilic carbon, the first toxicophore category on slide 30; the oxygen ends up as an alcohol on the drug, not on the protein. Free-radical bioactivation is a separate category, with isoniazid and phenytoin as the examples on slides 39 and 40.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxDefense,
 teachImg:'tx_epoxide_cbz',
 teach:[
  {h:'What the slide draws',
   t:'The top row is generic: an alkene is oxidised by a CYP to an epoxide, a protein nucleophile attacks one carbon of the ring, and the product carries the nucleophile on that carbon and a hydroxyl on the other. The bottom row is carbamazepine: CYP3A4 makes the 10,11-epoxide, marked in red, and the slide leaves the next step as a question mark for the class to fill in.'},
  {h:'Where the reaction goes instead, when the defense is working',
   t:'The question mark has two safe answers, written on the slide by hand as EH and GST. Epoxide hydrolase opens the ring with water, giving a diol, and glutathione S-transferase opens it with the glutathione thiol; either way the electrophilic carbon has reacted with something harmless before it reaches a protein. At an ordinary dose the two keep pace with CYP3A4. When they are outrun, the adduct on the top row is the result.'}],
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 33; toxicophore categories slide 30; enzyme defense slide 60',
 quote:'Bioactivated electrophilic carbons – Example: Epoxide Carbons'},

{id:'tx-7', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'bap-bay-region',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'both',
 stem:'Benzo[a]pyrene from smoked and barbecued food becomes carcinogenic after bioactivation. What is formed?',
 options:[
  {t:'An epoxide whose electrophilic carbons form DNA adducts', correct:true,
   why:'Staged CYP oxidation converts benzo[a]pyrene to benzo[a]pyrene diol epoxide, abbreviated BPDE. The epoxide carbons are electron poor, and the flat aromatic system sits against DNA, so the adduct forms directly on the bases and the mutation follows.'},
  {t:'A stable glucuronide that accumulates in liver', correct:false,
   why:'A glucuronide is the detoxified, water-soluble endpoint prepared for elimination. Conjugation of this kind is what prevents the carcinogenic outcome rather than causing it.'},
  {t:'A free radical that depletes glutathione only', correct:false,
   why:'Radicals are a real toxicophore category, slide 30, with isoniazid and phenytoin as the lecture\'s examples. Benzo[a]pyrene is taught as an epoxide and DNA adduct mechanism instead.'},
  {t:'An aldehyde that forms a hapten complex', correct:false,
   why:'That is the ethanol pathway on slide 42, where acetaldehyde complexes with protein and provokes an immune response. The endpoint there is hypersensitivity rather than a DNA mutation.'}],
 teachImg:'rx_bap_bpde',
 teach:'Procarcinogens are harmless until metabolism makes them dangerous, which is why the CYP families that handle flat aromatic compounds appear in both halves of this course. Benzo[a]pyrene reaches the body from smoke and charred food and is oxidized in stages to BPDE, which binds covalently to DNA. Aflatoxin B1 on slide 34 follows the same shape, arriving in moldy corn and peanuts and binding DNA as a fungal hepatocarcinogen. In both, the covalent DNA bond is the carcinogenic event, because a permanently modified base can be miscopied at the next replication. This is the mechanism behind the bioactivation tables in MCMet-9_15_2026.pdf, where CYP1A1 and CYP1A2 handle the polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 34–35',
 quote:'B[a]P – pro-carcinogen in smoke, charred foods, etc. Metabolized to BPDE which covalently binds to DNA'},

{id:'tx-8', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'parallel-double-bonds',
 tags:['structure','mechanism','exam-pattern'], source:'both',
 stem:'Which structural feature signals an electrophilic double bond toxicophore?',
 options:[
  {t:'Parallel conjugated double bonds, as in a quinone-imine', correct:true,
   why:'Conjugation spreads electron density across the system and leaves particular carbons short of it. Those carbons then accept a nucleophile in a conjugate addition, which is how a quinone-imine such as NAPQI binds protein.'},
  {t:'An isolated double bond in a long alkyl chain', correct:false,
   why:'A lone alkene has nothing to conjugate with, so no carbon is left electron poor. Simple alkenes are treated as metabolically stable in MCMet-9_16_2026.pdf and are largely excreted unchanged.'},
  {t:'A carbon-nitrogen single bond in an amine', correct:false,
   why:'An amine nitrogen carries a lone pair, making it nucleophilic. That is the opposite electronic character, and it puts amines on the receiving end of this chemistry as targets rather than as toxicophores.'},
  {t:'A saturated ring system', correct:false,
   why:'Saturation removes the pi system entirely, so there is no conjugation to create an electron-poor centre. A saturated ring is one of the shapes a drug is given to avoid this reactivity.'}],
 svg:DIA.cisTransEpox,
 teachImg:'tx_michael',
 teach:'Conjugated double bonds are worth recognising on sight because three separate toxicities in this lecture run through them. NAPQI from acetaminophen carries the pattern as a quinone-imine, slide 49. The clozapine metabolite behind agranulocytosis is a reactive imine that binds neutrophils covalently, slide 54. The diclofenac intermediates on slides 37 and 38 are the third. In each case the chemistry is the same: a nucleophile adds to an electron-poor carbon in the conjugated system and the bond is permanent. Seeing the pattern also predicts the defense, since glutathione is a nucleophile and can add in the same way, which is exactly how N-acetylcysteine works as the acetaminophen antidote.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 37–38',
 quote:'Bioactivated electrophilic double bond intermediates',
 note:'The same pattern explains three separate toxicities in this lecture: NAPQI from acetaminophen, the diclofenac intermediates, and the clozapine metabolite behind agranulocytosis.'},

{id:'tx-9', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'hapten-definition',
 tags:['definition','mechanism'], source:'both',
 stem:'What is a hapten in the toxicity context?',
 options:[
  {t:'A small molecule that binds a protein, and the resulting complex triggers an immune response', correct:true,
   why:'Neither component provokes a response alone. The small molecule is too small to be recognised, and the protein is the body\'s own. Only the complex is seen as foreign, which is how an ordinary drug fragment ends up causing hypersensitivity.'},
  {t:'A large protein that binds a drug and inactivates it', correct:false,
   why:'The roles are reversed. The hapten is the small molecule; the protein is the carrier it attaches to. Protein binding that inactivates a drug is a distribution phenomenon and provokes no immune response.'},
  {t:'A reactive oxygen species that damages membranes', correct:false,
   why:'Reactive oxygen species belong to the free radical category on slides 30 and 41 and damage cells directly rather than through the immune system.'},
  {t:'A metabolite that is excreted unchanged', correct:false,
   why:'An unreactive metabolite on its way out poses no risk. Hapten formation requires the metabolite to be reactive enough to attach itself to a protein first.'}],
 teachImg:'rx_ethanol_hapten',
 teach:'Hapten formation is how covalent binding produces an immune injury rather than a direct one, and it explains the hypersensitivity entry on the causes list. The sequence is the same as for any adduct, a reactive species binding a protein, but the consequence differs: the altered protein is presented as foreign and the immune system responds, so the damage is done by the patient\'s own defenses. That also explains why these reactions are unpredictable from dose, since they depend on immune recognition rather than on concentration. Ethanol and urushiol, the poison ivy compound, are the lecture\'s examples on slide 42, and sulfamethoxazole follows the same route on slide 58, where CYP metabolism yields nitroso haptens that trigger a skin reaction.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 42',
 quote:'Hapten-immunogens can trigger hepatic hypersensitivity'},

{id:'tx-10', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'ethanol-two-haptens',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'transcript',
 stem:'Ethanol generates two haptens implicated in liver cirrhosis. What are they and how is each formed?',
 options:[
  {t:'Acetaldehyde from alcohol dehydrogenase, and an ethoxy radical from CYP2E1', correct:true,
   why:'Two routes act on the same molecule. Alcohol dehydrogenase oxidizes ethanol to acetaldehyde, an electrophilic carbonyl. CYP2E1, which ethanol itself induces, produces a radical species. Either can attach to protein and create the complex the immune system responds to.'},
  {t:'Acetaldehyde and acetic acid, both from aldehyde dehydrogenase', correct:false,
   why:'Half of this is right in the wrong place. Aldehyde dehydrogenase converts acetaldehyde onward to acetate, which is the harmless endpoint of the pathway rather than a second reactive species; acetaldehyde itself comes from alcohol dehydrogenase one step earlier.'},
  {t:'Ethyl glucuronide and ethyl sulfate, from UDP-glucuronosyltransferase and sulfotransferase', correct:false,
   why:'These are genuine ethanol conjugates and are used as markers of drinking, but they are detoxified Phase 2 products formed for elimination and carry no reactive centre.'},
  {t:'Ethylene glycol and oxalic acid', correct:false,
   why:'Those belong to the ethylene glycol nephrotoxicity case on slide 57, a different molecule with a different endpoint: crystal obstruction in the nephron rather than an immune reaction in the liver.'}],
 teachImg:'rx_ethanol_hapten',
 teach:'Ethanol shows how one small molecule can reach the same endpoint by two independent routes. Alcohol dehydrogenase gives acetaldehyde, which is an electrophilic carbonyl and reacts with protein directly. CYP2E1 gives a radical, and because chronic drinking induces CYP2E1 the second route grows with continued exposure. Both products form hapten-protein complexes, and the immune response to them contributes to cirrhosis. Slide 47 lists alcohol use as a hepatotoxicity risk factor for two reasons, cirrhosis and glutathione depletion. The CYP2E1 side comes from two other slides: MCMet-9_15_2026.pdf slide 28 names ethanol as a 2E1 inducer, and slide 49 of this lecture prints 2E1/1A2/3A4 on the arrow that turns acetaminophen into NAPQI. Together they explain the acetaminophen risk: more CYP2E1 means more NAPQI, while less glutathione means less capacity to remove it.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 42',
 quote:'Example: Ethanol, Urushiol (in poison ivy) can form hapten- protein immunogens',
 note:'The slide 42 figure prints both: ADH gives CH3CHO, acetaldehyde, and 2E1 gives C2H5O•, the ethoxy radical. The 9/17 lecture named them aloud as well. The slide text names ethanol and urushiol as hapten formers without listing ethanol\'s two routes.'},

{id:'tx-11', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'hep', concept:'apap-napqi',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'both',
 stem:'At normal doses acetaminophen is safe, but in overdose it becomes hepatotoxic. What changes?',
 options:[
  {t:'Sulfation and glucuronidation are exhausted, so CYPs bioactivate the excess to NAPQI', correct:true,
   why:'At normal doses sulfates and glucuronides are the major metabolites and the drug leaves without incident. Those Phase 2 routes are saturable, so above a threshold the excess is diverted into Phase 1 oxidation, which yields N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine, a quinone-imine carrying the conjugated double bond pattern.'},
  {t:'The drug begins to be absorbed by a different intestinal transporter', correct:false,
   why:'Nothing about absorption changes, and the drug is well absorbed at both doses. The shift happens after absorption, in which metabolic route carries the load.'},
  {t:'Renal elimination stops and the drug accumulates unchanged', correct:false,
   why:'The injury is caused by a reactive metabolite rather than by parent drug building up. Accumulation of intact drug is the amiodarone mechanism on slide 55, where the toxicity follows the drug itself into lung tissue.'},
  {t:'Acetaminophen directly denatures hepatic proteins at high concentration', correct:false,
   why:'The parent molecule is not reactive, which is why the drug is safe below 4 grams a day. Bioactivation is a required step, and that is exactly why the antidote works by intercepting the metabolite rather than by removing the parent.'}],
 svg:DIA.apapNapqi,
 teachImg:'tx_apap_slide',
 teach:'This is the clearest case of a saturable protective route in the course, and the threshold behaviour follows from it. Safe below about 4 grams a day, hepatotoxic above, because sulfation and glucuronidation have a ceiling and Phase 1 oxidation does not. Once NAPQI is formed, glutathione removes it, so a second threshold applies: injury begins when NAPQI production outruns glutathione supply. That explains why the risk factors compound the way they do. Alcohol use induces CYP2E1, raising NAPQI production, and depletes glutathione, lowering removal, which is why the same dose is more dangerous in a heavy drinker. The antidote restores the missing nucleophile rather than blocking the CYP.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 49',
 quote:'Overdose: APAP exhausts UGTs/STs, and undergoes phase I bioactivation to NAPQI'},

{id:'tx-12', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'def', concept:'nac-antidote',
 tags:['case','antidote'], source:'both',
 stem:'What is the antidote for acetaminophen toxicity and how does it work?',
 options:[
  {t:'N-acetylcysteine, which mimics glutathione and its nucleophilic thiol attacks NAPQI', correct:true,
   why:'N-acetylcysteine supplies a sulfhydryl group, the same nucleophile glutathione offers. It adds across the electrophilic double bond of NAPQI to give a water-soluble conjugate that leaves in urine, so it substitutes for glutathione once those stores are spent.'},
  {t:'Pralidoxime, which reactivates the inhibited enzyme', correct:false,
   why:'Pralidoxime is the organophosphate antidote on slide 64, where it regenerates the cholinesterases. It is built for a phosphorylated enzyme, not for a free reactive metabolite in solution.'},
  {t:'Sodium bicarbonate, which traps the drug in urine', correct:false,
   why:'That is the aspirin overdose strategy on slide 15, which works because salicylate is an acid with an ionizable group. It acts on renal handling of intact drug and does nothing about a reactive metabolite already formed in the liver.'},
  {t:'Allopurinol, which inhibits xanthine oxidase', correct:false,
   why:'Allopurinol does inhibit xanthine oxidase, which oxidises purines to uric acid, on MCMet-9_16_2026.pdf slide 24, so the pairing itself is correct and it is the anti-gout drug named there. What makes it wrong here is that the injury and the remedy have to match: acetaminophen toxicity is a depleted-nucleophile problem, so the treatment must supply a thiol. Purine metabolism never touches N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine. Choosing this means matching a familiar enzyme name to the word antidote instead of asking what the damage actually is.'}],
 svg:DIA.apapNapqi,
 teachImg:'tx_nac',
 teach:'The antidote follows from the chemistry of the injury. NAPQI is an electrophile and glutathione normally removes it by offering a thiol; in overdose the glutathione runs out, so the treatment supplies a replacement thiol rather than interfering with the enzymes. This is the general form of electrophile defense on slide 60, where glutathione S-transferases and epoxide hydrolases deactivate reactive intermediates, applied as therapy. It also explains the timing: the antidote is useful while NAPQI is still being produced and before adducts have formed, so it works best early. Contrast pralidoxime, which reverses an existing covalent modification but only before aging makes that bond permanent.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 63',
 quote:'NAC - antidote for APAP toxicity · Electrophile deactivation by NAC'},

{id:'tx-13', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'def', concept:'2pam-organophosphate',
 tags:['case','antidote','mechanism'], source:'both',
 stem:'Pralidoxime (2-PAM) is given for organophosphate poisoning. How does it work?',
 options:[
  {t:'It cleaves the organophosphate off the cholinesterases and regenerates the enzyme, provided the phosphorylated enzyme has not yet aged', correct:true,
   why:'Pralidoxime is an oxime, a nucleophile strong enough to attack the phosphorus holding the enzyme and pull the organophosphate off, restoring both acetylcholinesterase and butyrylcholinesterase. Aging converts that bond into one the oxime can no longer break, so the window closes.'},
  {t:'It blocks acetylcholine receptors to prevent cholinergic crisis', correct:false,
   why:'Blocking the receptor treats the symptoms of excess acetylcholine, which is what atropine does, and it leaves the disabled enzyme disabled. Pralidoxime acts on the enzyme itself, so it addresses the cause.'},
  {t:'It supplies glutathione to conjugate the organophosphate', correct:false,
   why:'That is how N-acetylcysteine works in acetaminophen toxicity, slide 63, where the target is a free electrophile in solution. Here the problem is a covalent modification already attached to an enzyme, which needs removing rather than conjugating.'},
  {t:'It induces CYP enzymes to speed organophosphate clearance', correct:false,
   why:'Induction develops over days, from MCMet-9_15_2026.pdf, and a cholinergic crisis unfolds in minutes to hours. Nothing that relies on making new protein is useful on that timescale.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxDefense,
 teachImg:'tx_2pam',
 teach:'Pralidoxime is the one antidote in this lecture that reverses damage already done rather than preventing more, and the two limits named alongside it both follow from its structure. It is a charged oxime, so it does not cross the blood-brain barrier and cannot rescue cholinesterase in the central nervous system. It works by nucleophilic attack on the phosphorylated enzyme, so it depends on that bond still being breakable, and aging changes the bond chemically to a form it cannot attack. The contrast with N-acetylcysteine is the useful one: one antidote intercepts a reactive species before it binds, the other unbinds it afterwards, and each is bounded by a different clock.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 64',
 quote:'An Oxime used to regenerate ChEs (AChE and BuChE) · Disadvantages: 1) non-BBB penetrant; 2) admin. before "aging"'},

{id:'tx-14', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'def', concept:'antioxidant-enzymes',
 tags:['recall','enzyme'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which enzyme reduces the superoxide anion to hydrogen peroxide?',
 options:[
  {t:'Superoxide dismutase', correct:true,
   why:'Superoxide dismutase takes the superoxide radical anion and converts it to hydrogen peroxide, which is the first step of the oxidative stress defense. Hydrogen peroxide is still reactive, so the sequence continues rather than ending here.'},
  {t:'Catalase', correct:false,
   why:'Catalase handles the product of the previous step, breaking hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen. It is the second enzyme in the same chain, which is why the two are usually named together.'},
  {t:'Glutathione peroxidase', correct:false,
   why:'Glutathione peroxidase reduces peroxides, including lipid peroxides formed when radicals attack membranes, using glutathione as the reducing agent. It defends against a later consequence of radical damage rather than against superoxide itself.'},
  {t:'Glutathione reductase', correct:false,
   why:'Glutathione reductase does not act on a reactive oxygen species at all. It restores oxidized glutathione to its reduced form, keeping the supply available for the enzymes that do.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxDefense,
 teachImg:'tx_ros',
 teach:'Oxidative stress defense is a chain, and each enzyme hands its product to the next rather than acting alone. Superoxide dismutase converts the superoxide radical to hydrogen peroxide; catalase converts hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen; glutathione peroxidase handles peroxides that escape, and glutathione reductase regenerates the glutathione those reactions consume. Because it is a chain, a shortage at any point leaves reactive species surviving longer, which is one reason glutathione depletion appears so often as a risk factor. This arm sits beside the electrophile defense of slide 60: glutathione S-transferases and epoxide hydrolases deal with electrophiles, the antioxidant enzymes deal with radicals.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 61',
 quote:'Enzyme Defense- Oxidative Stress Defense by antioxidant enzymes'},

{id:'tx-15', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'def', concept:'gst-eh-defense',
 tags:['reasoning','enzyme'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which two enzymes provide the front-line defense against electrophilic intermediates?',
 options:[
  {t:'Glutathione S-transferases and epoxide hydrolases', correct:true,
   why:'Glutathione S-transferase attaches glutathione to an electrophilic carbon, and epoxide hydrolase adds water to open a strained epoxide into a diol. Both convert a reactive centre into an unreactive one before it can reach protein or DNA.'},
  {t:'Catalase and superoxide dismutase', correct:false,
   why:'These defend the other arm, against reactive oxygen species rather than electrophiles, slide 61. Both arms are needed because radicals and electrophiles damage cells by different chemistry.'},
  {t:'UDP-glucuronosyltransferase and sulfotransferase', correct:false,
   why:'These conjugate electron-rich groups such as hydroxyls to make a drug polar enough to eliminate. That is elimination chemistry, and it is the capacity whose exhaustion starts acetaminophen toxicity, slide 49, rather than the defense against the electrophile that follows.'},
  {t:'Alcohol and aldehyde dehydrogenases', correct:false,
   why:'These are Phase 1 oxidases, and in this lecture they appear on the hazard side: they convert ethanol to the reactive acetaldehyde on slide 42 and ethylene glycol to its toxic acids on slide 57.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxDefense,
 teachImg:'tx_defense1',
 teach:'Electrophile defense works by getting there first. Both enzymes present the reactive centre with a harmless partner before a protein or DNA nucleophile finds it: glutathione S-transferase supplies a thiol, epoxide hydrolase supplies water. Because both are enzymes with finite capacity, the defense can be exceeded by dose and weakened by anything that lowers glutathione, which is why alcohol use appears as a hepatotoxicity risk factor on slide 47. The same chemistry becomes therapy when glutathione runs out, since N-acetylcysteine offers the missing thiol directly, slide 63. Which enzyme applies follows from the toxicophore: epoxide hydrolase for an epoxide, glutathione S-transferase for electrophilic carbons and conjugated double bonds alike.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 60',
 quote:'Enzyme Defense- Electrophile deactivation by GSTs and EHs'},

{id:'tx-16', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'extra', concept:'terfenadine-herg',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'both',
 stem:'Terfenadine was withdrawn while its metabolite fexofenadine remains on the market. Why?',
 options:[
  {t:'The parent drug inhibits the hERG potassium channel and causes arrhythmias; the metabolite keeps the antihistamine effect without that inhibition', correct:true,
   why:'The hazard here belongs to the parent rather than to a metabolite, which reverses the usual pattern in this lecture. Terfenadine accumulating rather than being cleared reaches concentrations at which it acts off target on the cardiac hERG channel, and the result is arrhythmia.'},
  {t:'The metabolite is more potent as an antihistamine', correct:false,
   why:'Potency is not what separated them. Both act as antihistamines; the difference that mattered was cardiac safety, so the metabolite was developed as a drug in its own right.'},
  {t:'Terfenadine was hepatotoxic through a reactive epoxide', correct:false,
   why:'That mechanism belongs to the electrophile cases such as carbamazepine on slide 33. Terfenadine toxicity is receptor-mediated and cardiac, with no covalent adduct involved, which is why studying its metabolism produced a safer drug rather than a safer dose.'},
  {t:'Terfenadine could not be absorbed orally', correct:false,
   why:'It was absorbed and effective. The problem appeared when its clearance was blocked, since accumulation of the parent is precisely what produced the off-target effect.'}],
 teachImg:'tx_herg',
 teach:'Two lessons sit in this case. The first is that toxicity does not always come from a reactive metabolite: here the parent is the dangerous species and metabolism is protective, so anything that slows that metabolism raises risk. That is why terfenadine became dangerous in combination with CYP3A4 inhibitors, the class covered in MCMet-9_15_2026.pdf. The second is that understanding the metabolism produced the remedy, since the active metabolite was already an effective antihistamine without the hERG activity and could be marketed on its own. Off-target pharmacology of this kind is reversible binding rather than covalent modification, which is what distinguishes it from the adduct mechanisms in this lecture.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 53',
 quote:'Parent Drug Accum. → off target pharmacology · Interactions with hERG = Cardiac Arrythmias'},

{id:'tx-17', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'extra', concept:'doxorubicin-cardiotoxicity',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What mechanism underlies doxorubicin cardiotoxicity?',
 options:[
  {t:'Oxidation to radical species that generate reactive oxygen species in cardiac tissue', correct:true,
   why:'The anthracycline is converted to radical species, and mitochondrial iron helps propagate further reactive oxygen species. Cardiac muscle is dense in mitochondria and comparatively poor in antioxidant defense, so it takes the damage, and congestive heart failure can follow within 2 to 3 days.'},
  {t:'Direct hERG potassium channel blockade by the parent drug', correct:false,
   why:'That is the terfenadine mechanism on slide 53. Both are cardiotoxicity, which is why they are easy to confuse, but one is reversible channel binding and this one is radical damage to tissue.'},
  {t:'Covalent binding of an imine to neutrophils', correct:false,
   why:'That is clozapine hematotoxicity on slide 54, where the target is white blood cells rather than cardiac muscle.'},
  {t:'Accumulation of drug and metabolite in lung tissue', correct:false,
   why:'That is amiodarone on slide 55, where high lipophilicity drives accumulation. Amiodarone is also a cardiac drug, which makes the pairing worth keeping straight: its toxicity is pulmonary, while doxorubicin is an anticancer drug whose toxicity is cardiac.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teachImg:'tx_doxorubicin',
 teach:'Extrahepatic toxicity is organised by organ in this lecture, and each organ is vulnerable for a structural reason rather than at random. The heart appears twice with different mechanisms: doxorubicin through radicals and mitochondrial iron, terfenadine through hERG channel inhibition. Cardiac tissue is susceptible to the first because it is mitochondria-rich, and to the second because the hERG channel governs repolarisation there. Reading each case as organ plus mechanism keeps the list usable, since the same drug classes recur: blood for clozapine, lung for amiodarone, neurons for MPTP, kidney for ethylene glycol, and skin for sulfamethoxazole.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 52',
 quote:'Cardiotoxicity (due to mitochondrial Fe & ROS, etc.) · Doxorubicin (Anticancer Anthracycline) · Induces Congestive Heart Failure (CHF) in 2-3 days'},

{id:'tx-18', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'extra', concept:'clozapine-agranulocytosis',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'both',
 stem:'Clozapine requires blood count monitoring. What is the mechanism of its hematotoxicity?',
 options:[
  {t:'Biotransformation to a reactive imine bearing parallel double bonds, which binds covalently to neutrophils', correct:true,
   why:'The imine carries the conjugated double bond pattern, so a nucleophile on the neutrophil adds to it and the bond is permanent. Losing neutrophils produces agranulocytosis, a fall in white cell count, which is why the blood count is monitored rather than the drug concentration.'},
  {t:'Direct bone marrow suppression by the parent drug', correct:false,
   why:'Marrow suppression by a parent drug is a real class of hematotoxicity, but here the parent is not the reactive species. Bioactivation to the imine is required, which is why the reaction is not seen in every patient and why deactivating capacity matters.'},
  {t:'Immune complex formation with red cell membranes', correct:false,
   why:'Two details are wrong. The binding described is covalent rather than an immune complex, and the target is neutrophils rather than red cells, which is what makes the count that falls a white cell count.'},
  {t:'Inhibition of folate metabolism', correct:false,
   why:'Folate antagonism causes its own hematological problems, but it is not the mechanism taught here and would not produce the selective loss of neutrophils described.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teachImg:'tx_clozapine',
 teach:'This is the conjugated double bond pattern of slide 30 appearing in a specific tissue, so the chemistry connects directly to NAPQI and the diclofenac intermediates. What differs is the target: neutrophils rather than hepatocytes, and therefore a blood count rather than liver enzymes as the monitoring measure. Glutathione deactivates some of the reactive imine, which is why the reaction depends on the patient rather than occurring in everyone at the same dose, and it places clozapine alongside the other cases where defense capacity decides the outcome. Monitoring exists because the injury is detectable before it becomes dangerous, which is not true of every mechanism in this lecture.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 54',
 quote:'Hematotoxicity (Imine covalently binds to neutrophils) · Agranulocytocytosis (leukopenia - ↓ white blood cells)'},

{id:'tx-19', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'extra', concept:'amiodarone-pulmonary',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'both',
 stem:'Amiodarone causes pulmonary toxicity. Which property of the drug explains it?',
 options:[
  {t:'Very high lipophilicity, so both drug and metabolite distribute widely and accumulate in lung tissue', correct:true,
   why:'Amiodarone has a calculated log P of 9, which is extreme for a marketed drug. A molecule that lipophilic partitions into every lipid-rich compartment and leaves slowly, and its des-ethyl metabolite accumulates alongside it, so the tissue burden builds with continued dosing.'},
  {t:'It is a strong acid trapped in alveolar fluid', correct:false,
   why:'Ion trapping is a renal phenomenon in this course, slide 14, and it depends on an ionizable group and a pH difference across a membrane. Amiodarone is taught here for its lipophilicity, not as an acid.'},
  {t:'It generates an epoxide that binds pulmonary DNA', correct:false,
   why:'No bioactivation step is involved. The toxicity is accumulation of intact drug and metabolite, which is why it develops with cumulative exposure rather than after a single large dose.'},
  {t:'It is eliminated only by exhalation', correct:false,
   why:'Exhalation carries volatile agents, slide 22, which are small and readily enter the gas phase. Amiodarone is the opposite case: so lipophilic that it is retained rather than excreted by any route quickly.'}],
 teachImg:'tx_amiodarone',
 teach:'This case links the physicochemical half of the course to the toxicity half. Log P predicts where a molecule goes, and a value of 9 predicts that it goes almost everywhere lipid-rich and stays. No reactive intermediate is required for harm, because the drug itself accumulates to concentrations the tissue cannot tolerate, and the metabolite does the same. That makes the toxicity a function of cumulative dose and time rather than of a single exposure, and it makes the drug hard to stop quickly, since a large tissue reservoir empties slowly. It also contrasts usefully with doxorubicin: both are dose-related, but one is driven by distribution and the other by reactive chemistry.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 55',
 quote:'Amiodarone (antiarrhythmic drug, cLogP=9) · Toxicity - excessive Amiodarone & des-ethyl metabolite in lung tissue'},

{id:'tx-20', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'extra', concept:'ethylene-glycol-nephrotoxicity',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'both',
 stem:'Ethylene glycol causes nephrotoxicity. What is the sequence?',
 options:[
  {t:'Alcohol and aldehyde dehydrogenases oxidize it to aldehydes and acids, ending in oxalic acid, which forms calcium oxalate deposits that block nephrons', correct:true,
   why:'The parent is a sweet, odourless liquid and is comparatively harmless as given. Alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase oxidize it in stages to toxic species ending in oxalic acid, which binds calcium and precipitates as crystals that physically obstruct the tubules.'},
  {t:'It is a direct hERG channel blocker causing renal arrhythmia', correct:false,
   why:'hERG is a cardiac potassium channel and belongs to terfenadine on slide 53. The term arrhythmia does not apply to the kidney at all.'},
  {t:'It forms an epoxide that binds renal DNA', correct:false,
   why:'No epoxide appears in this pathway, which runs through aldehydes to carboxylic acids. Epoxide formation and DNA binding belong to the procarcinogens on slides 34 and 35, where the endpoint is mutation rather than obstruction.'},
  {t:'It depletes renal glutathione, allowing free radical damage', correct:false,
   why:'Glutathione depletion is the acetaminophen mechanism on slide 49. Here the damage is physical rather than chemical: crystals block the nephron, and no reactive intermediate binds anything.'}],
 teachImg:'tx_nephro',
 teach:'This is bioactivation without an electrophile, which makes it a useful exception. The same two enzymes that detoxify ethanol, alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase, convert ethylene glycol into something worse, so an enzyme is protective or harmful according to the substrate rather than by nature. The endpoint is mechanical: calcium oxalate crystals obstruct nephrons, which is why the injury is renal and why it appears as deposits rather than adducts. The treatment logic follows directly, since blocking the first enzyme prevents the toxic products from forming at all. Poisonings occur both accidentally and deliberately, and the sweet taste and lack of odour are part of why.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 57',
 quote:'Ethylene Glycol (EG) · A sweet & odor-less liquid, metabolized by ADH & ALDH to toxic species'},

{id:'tx-21', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'hep', concept:'hepatotox-risk-factors',
 tags:['recall','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which of the following is a risk factor for drug-induced hepatotoxicity?',
 options:[
  {t:'Alcohol use, which induces cirrhosis and depletes glutathione', correct:true,
   why:'Two effects compound. Cirrhosis reduces functioning liver tissue, and glutathione depletion removes the nucleophile that deactivates reactive metabolites, so more reactive material survives in an organ less able to withstand it.'},
  {t:'Being male rather than female', correct:false,
   why:'The listed risk runs the other way, with women more affected than men, and acetaminophen, halothane, diclofenac and sulindac named as the drugs where that difference shows.'},
  {t:'Rapid renal clearance of the parent drug', correct:false,
   why:'Efficient renal clearance removes drug before the liver is further exposed. The pharmacokinetic entry on the risk list points the opposite way, toward sustained-release and long-acting drugs and polypharmacy, all of which extend exposure.'},
  {t:'Low plasma protein binding alone', correct:false,
   why:'Binding governs how much free drug is available and is not listed as a hepatotoxicity risk in its own right. Where distribution does matter it enters through the drug interactions entry on the causes list, slide 29.'}],
 teach:'Drug-induced hepatotoxicity is the leading cause of liver injury and more than 600 drugs are hepatotoxic, so the risk factors are the practical part of this section. They group into three kinds. Capacity factors decide how much reactive material is removed: genetics through metabolizer phenotype, alcohol use through glutathione depletion, and existing liver disease. Exposure factors decide how much is produced: pharmacokinetics such as sustained-release and long-acting formulations and polypharmacy, and pharmacodynamics such as overdose and narrow therapeutic index drugs. Patient factors cover age, with the elderly vulnerable through reduced clearance, liver mass and blood flow, and sex, with women more affected. Herbal remedies are listed separately because they are taken unreported.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slides 46–47',
 quote:'Alcohol use: Induces liver cirrhosis, GSH depletion'},

{id:'tx-22', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'hep', concept:'nitrofurantoin-metabolites',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which reactive intermediates are implicated in nitrofurantoin hepatotoxicity?',
 options:[
  {t:'Nitroso and hydroxylamine intermediates', correct:true,
   why:'Reduction of the nitro group does not go straight to the amine. It passes through nitroso and hydroxylamine species, both reactive enough to form protein adducts, and the hydroxylamine is the same N-hydroxy group listed among the toxicophores on slide 30.'},
  {t:'A quinone-imine with parallel double bonds', correct:false,
   why:'That is NAPQI from acetaminophen on slide 49. Both are hepatotoxic reactive metabolites, but the quinone-imine arises by oxidation of a phenol rather than by reduction of a nitro group.'},
  {t:'A bay-region epoxide', correct:false,
   why:'That belongs to benzo[a]pyrene on slide 35, where staged oxidation gives BPDE and the endpoint is a DNA adduct and carcinogenesis rather than direct liver injury.'},
  {t:'An acyl glucuronide', correct:false,
   why:'A glucuronide is a Phase 2 conjugate formed to end reactivity and promote elimination, so naming one as the reactive intermediate inverts its role.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teachImg:'tx_nitrofurantoin',
 teach:'Nitro group reduction is a stepwise pathway, and the intermediate stages are where the hazard sits rather than the endpoint. Nitro becomes nitroso, then hydroxylamine, then amine, and the two middle species are reactive enough to bind protein. That connects this case to two others: the N-hydroxy toxicophore on slide 44, where N-hydroxylation of an aromatic amine followed by sulfation produces a carcinogen, and azo reduction on slide 43, which also releases aromatic amines. Two of the three are reductions: the nitro group of nitrofurantoin and the azo bond on slide 43 are both reduced, which sets them apart from most of the bioactivation in this course. The AAF case runs the other way: CYP1A2 oxidises the amine nitrogen to the N-hydroxy group, and sulfation of that N-OH gives the carcinogen. What the three share is a reactive nitrogen species, not the direction of the reaction that makes it.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 48',
 quote:'Nitrofurantoin (NFT) Toxicity - Nitroso & hydroxylamine intermediates'},

{id:'tx-23', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'hep', concept:'nitrosamine-ndsri',
 tags:['recall','case'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Nitrosamine drug substance related impurities (NDSRIs) became a regulatory concern. Which drug product is the example?',
 options:[
  {t:'Ranitidine, withdrawn over NDMA contamination', correct:true,
   why:'N-nitrosodimethylamine is hepatotoxic and implicated in other cancers, and it was found in ranitidine products. The hazard is unusual in this lecture because it comes from the manufactured product rather than from what the body does to the drug.'},
  {t:'Acetaminophen, withdrawn over NAPQI', correct:false,
   why:'NAPQI is formed in the patient during overdose rather than present in the product, and acetaminophen remains marketed and safe below about 4 grams a day, slide 49.'},
  {t:'Terfenadine, withdrawn over hERG inhibition', correct:false,
   why:'Terfenadine was indeed withdrawn, which makes this the closest wrong answer, but for cardiac arrhythmia from off-target channel binding, slide 53, not for an impurity.'},
  {t:'Clozapine, restricted over agranulocytosis', correct:false,
   why:'Clozapine is restricted and monitored rather than withdrawn, and the cause is a reactive metabolite formed in the patient, slide 54.'}],
 teachImg:'tx_ndsri',
 teach:'Nitrosamine impurities are a different kind of toxicity from everything else in this lecture, because the reactive species never had to be made by the patient. It arrives in the tablet, formed during manufacture or on storage from an amine in the drug substance itself, which is what the phrase drug substance related impurity records. That changes where the control has to sit: the pharmacology of the drug is unchanged and no dose adjustment helps, so the response is at the manufacturing and regulatory level rather than the prescribing level. N-nitrosodimethylamine in ranitidine is the example, hepatotoxic and linked to other cancers. Slide 50 also draws how it does the damage: CYP2E1, or a non-enzymatic step, converts NDMA to the methyldiazonium ion, an electrophile that transfers a methyl group onto a cellular nucleophile, drawn as Nu-CH3.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 50',
 quote:'N-Nitrosodimethylamine (NDMA) in Ranitidine products · Hepatotoxic and implicated in other cancers · NDMA → (2E1/non-enzymatic) → reactive methyldiazonium ion → Nu-CH3'},

{id:'tx-24', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'free-radical-teratogen',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Phenytoin is implicated in teratogenic outcomes. What mechanism is given?',
 options:[
  {t:'A free radical intermediate that propagates further radicals and damages cells', correct:true,
   why:'A radical carries an unpaired electron, so reacting with a stable molecule creates another radical rather than ending the reaction. One bioactivation event therefore produces a chain, and damage occurring during fetal development gives the teratogenic outcome.'},
  {t:'Covalent binding of an epoxide to fetal DNA', correct:false,
   why:'Epoxide adducts belong to carbamazepine on slide 33 and to the procarcinogens on slides 34 and 35. Both are electrophile mechanisms, where one reactive molecule forms one adduct, rather than the self-propagating chain described for a radical.'},
  {t:'Depletion of maternal glutathione stores only', correct:false,
   why:'Glutathione depletion is part of the acetaminophen picture on slide 49 and a hepatotoxicity risk factor on slide 47. It can worsen radical damage by removing a defense, but it is not itself the mechanism given for phenytoin teratogenicity.'},
  {t:'Inhibition of fetal CYP3A7', correct:false,
   why:'CYP3A7 is the fetal isoform named in MCMet-9_15_2026.pdf, so the name is real, but no inhibition mechanism of this kind is presented for phenytoin.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teachImg:'tx_phenytoin',
 teach:'Radicals differ from electrophiles in arithmetic as much as in chemistry. An electrophile is consumed when it forms an adduct, so the damage is proportional to how much was made. A radical creates a new radical each time it reacts, so a small amount propagates until something terminates the chain, which is why antioxidant enzymes exist as a separate defense arm on slide 61. Phenytoin and isoniazid are the two radical examples here, and the endpoints differ, teratogenicity for one and host tissue damage for the other, because the tissue and timing differ rather than the chemistry.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 40; fetal CYP3A7 and susceptibility Lecture #14 (MCMet-9_16_2026.pdf) slide 45',
 quote:'Bioactivated free radical teratogenic intermediates · Example: Phenytoin'},

{id:'tx-25', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'isoniazid-radical',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Isoniazid kills mycobacteria through a reactive intermediate, and it can also injure the patient\'s liver. What produces the species that harms the host?',
 options:[
  {t:'Liver N-acetyltransferase converts isoniazid to acetylhydrazine, which loses nitrogen to give an acetyl radical', correct:true,
   why:'The right-hand arrow on slide 39: liver NAT acetylates the hydrazide, and the acetylhydrazine loses N2 to leave an acetyl radical, labelled host toxicity. The species that harms the patient is made by the patient\'s own Phase 2 enzyme.'},
  {t:'The radical made by the mycobacterial catalase-peroxidase leaks into host tissue', correct:false,
   why:'The left-hand arrow: the mycobacterial catalase-peroxidase converts isoniazid, with loss of N2, to the isonicotinoyl radical that inhibits DNA synthesis in the organism. That radical is made inside the bacterium by a bacterial enzyme; the host injury comes from a different path, the acetylation in the liver.'},
  {t:'The drug accumulates unchanged because it is not metabolised', correct:false,
   why:'Isoniazid is metabolised, and the metabolism is the problem: acetylation by NAT is the step that leads to the acetyl radical. N-acetyltransferase 2 is polymorphic, so the rate of that step differs between patients.'},
  {t:'A CYP makes an epoxide that binds host DNA', correct:false,
   why:'Epoxide formation and DNA binding belong to the procarcinogens on slides 34 and 35. Isoniazid is the free-radical example, the fourth toxicophore category on slide 30, and no epoxide appears on slide 39.'}],
 teachImg:'tx_inh_radical',
 teach:[
  {h:'Two arrows from one drug',
   t:'Slide 39 draws isoniazid in the middle with a reaction to each side. To the left, the mycobacterial catalase-peroxidase removes N2 and leaves the isonicotinoyl radical, which inhibits DNA synthesis in the organism: that is the drug working. To the right, liver N-acetyltransferase gives acetylhydrazine, which loses N2 to an acetyl radical: that is the host toxicity. Same hydrazide, two enzymes, two radicals, two targets.'},
  {h:'Why this is a bioactivated free radical',
   t:'Neither radical is present in the drug as given. Each is made by an enzyme, so isoniazid sits in the bioactivated free-radical category of slide 30, with phenytoin on slide 40 as the teratogenic example of the same category. The defense against a radical is the antioxidant set on slide 61, not the electrophile enzymes.'}],
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 39; categories slide 30; phenytoin slide 40; antioxidant defense slide 61',
 quote:'Bioactivated free radical reactive intermediates · Example: Isoniazid (INH) Anti-TB drug'},

{id:'tx-26', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'azo-reduction-carcinogen',
 tags:['case','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Why was the azo dye Solvent Red withdrawn from food use?',
 options:[
  {t:'Reduction of the azo group releases two aromatic amines, 1-amino-2-naphthol and o-anisidine, which are carcinogenic', correct:true,
   why:'Azo reduction splits the nitrogen-nitrogen double bond and releases two aromatic amines; Lecture 15 slide 43 draws the reaction, with the arrow labelled skin azo-reduction, and names the products 1-amino-2-naphthol and o-anisidine. Aromatic amines are the starting point for N-hydroxylation to carcinogens, slide 44, so the fragment rather than the intact dye is what causes the harm.'},
  {t:'It forms a quinone-imine in the liver', correct:false,
   why:'Quinone-imine formation is the acetaminophen pathway on slide 49, an oxidation of a phenol. Azo reduction is the opposite kind of reaction and produces fragments rather than a modified intact molecule.'},
  {t:'It chelates dietary iron', correct:false,
   why:'Iron appears in this lecture in the doxorubicin mechanism on slide 52, where mitochondrial iron propagates reactive oxygen species, and not as a chelation hazard from dyes.'},
  {t:'It inhibits xanthine oxidase and raises urate', correct:false,
   why:'Xanthine oxidase inhibition describes allopurinol and lowers urate rather than raising it, so both the enzyme and the direction are wrong here.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teachImg:'tx_azo_solventred',
 teach:'Azo reduction is the same reaction in both directions of usefulness, which is what makes it instructive. Azoreductases, in gut bacteria for sulfasalazine and, as slide 43 labels it, in skin for Solvent Red 1, split the azo bond and release two aromatic amines. In sulfasalazine that is exactly the intention, since it delivers the active fragment to the colon, and the reaction is the basis of the prodrug. In Solvent Red 1 the fragment released is carcinogenic, and the dye was banned from food. The chemistry does not change; what changes is which fragment is liberated, so an azo group is read as a site where the molecule will be cut rather than as good or bad in itself. The released amines then feed into the N-hydroxylation route on slide 44.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 43',
 quote:'Azo reduction to carcinogens · Example: Solvent Red 1 (a banned food dye) · Undergoes reduction to carcinogenic aromatic amines · 1-Amino-2-naphthol + o-Anisidine'},

{id:'tx-27', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'mech', concept:'n-hydroxylation-carcinogen',
 tags:['mechanism','structure'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Why are hydroxylamine groups rare in marketed drugs?',
 options:[
  {t:'N-hydroxylation of aromatic amines produces procarcinogens that can be further activated to DNA-binding species', correct:true,
   why:'The N-hydroxy group appears among the toxicophores on slide 30 for this reason. In 2-acetylaminofluorene the hydroxylamine is formed first and then sulfated, and it is the sulfate conjugate that becomes the carcinogen, so a Phase 2 reaction completes the activation rather than ending it.'},
  {t:'They are too polar to be absorbed orally', correct:false,
   why:'Polarity affects absorption, covered in the ADMET material, but it is not why this group is avoided. The concern is carcinogenic bioactivation rather than getting the drug into the body.'},
  {t:'They are hydrolyzed instantly by carboxylesterases', correct:false,
   why:'Carboxylesterases act on esters, amides, carbamates and carbonates, from MCMet-9_16_2026.pdf. A hydroxylamine offers none of those bonds.'},
  {t:'They cannot be conjugated by any Phase 2 enzyme', correct:false,
   why:'The opposite is true and is the problem: sulfation of the hydroxylamine is the step that produces the carcinogen in the acetylaminofluorene case.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxicophores,
 teachImg:'tx_noh_aaf',
 teach:'This case breaks the usual rule that Phase 2 conjugation ends reactivity. Sulfation normally adds a polar group and prepares a molecule to leave, but on a hydroxylamine the sulfate is a good leaving group, so its departure generates the electrophile that binds DNA. Conjugation therefore completes the activation rather than preventing it. That is why the N-hydroxy group is treated as a structural alert during drug design and why it is scarce in marketed drugs. It also links to the two reduction pathways in this lecture, since azo reduction on slide 43 and nitro reduction on slide 48 both deliver aromatic amines and hydroxylamines into this same route.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 44',
 quote:'N-hydroxylations to carcinogens · Example: Acetylaminofluorene (AAF) - N-OH AAF sulfation leads to the carcinogen'},

{id:'tx-28', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'causes', concept:'narrow-ti',
 tags:['reasoning','definition'], source:'both',
 stem:'Why do narrow therapeutic index drugs appear on the causes of toxicity list?',
 options:[
  {t:'The gap between an effective and a toxic concentration is small, so ordinary variation can push a patient into toxicity', correct:true,
   why:'Every patient varies in clearance, and a narrow window leaves no room to absorb that variation. An interaction, a poor metabolizer phenotype or reduced organ function shifts exposure by an amount that would be harmless for most drugs and is not harmless here.'},
  {t:'They are always prodrugs requiring bioactivation', correct:false,
   why:'Therapeutic index and prodrug status are independent. Warfarin is active as given and has a narrow index; codeine is a prodrug with a wide one.'},
  {t:'They are never metabolized, so they accumulate', correct:false,
   why:'Many are extensively metabolized, and that is precisely why interactions matter so much for them. If they were not metabolized, inhibitors and inducers would not move their concentrations at all.'},
  {t:'They have no reactive functional groups', correct:false,
   why:'Toxicophore content and therapeutic index are separate entries on the causes list, slide 29. A drug can have a narrow index with no reactive group, harming through exaggerated on-target effect rather than through chemistry.'}],
 teach:'The dose entry on the causes list covers two situations rather than one. The first is straightforward overdose, where too much drug is taken. The second is the narrow therapeutic index, where the dose is correct but the window is so tight that ordinary variation in clearance carries the patient out of it. The second is the harder problem clinically, because nothing was done wrong: the prescription was right and the patient took it as directed. It is also where the rest of this course does its work, since interactions, metabolizer phenotype and organ function are all ways clearance moves, and each matters in proportion to how narrow the window is.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 29',
 quote:'Dose (overdose, Narrow TI drugs, etc.)',
 note:'Slide 29 names the category without naming a drug; the narrow-index examples used elsewhere in the course are warfarin, phenytoin and digoxin.'},

{id:'tx-29', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'def', concept:'charcoal-timing',
 tags:['recall','case'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Oral activated charcoal is most useful within what window after ingestion?',
 options:[
  {t:'Within about one hour', correct:true,
   why:'Charcoal adsorbs drug that is still in the gastrointestinal tract, so it has to arrive while there is drug left to bind. Once absorption is complete the drug is in the circulation and charcoal in the lumen can no longer reach it.'},
  {t:'Within about 24 hours', correct:false,
   why:'By then most of an ingested dose has been absorbed. There is one exception worth separating out: for a drug that undergoes enterohepatic recycling, slide 18, charcoal remains useful later because drug keeps re-entering the intestine in bile.'},
  {t:'Only after dialysis has failed', correct:false,
   why:'These are not sequential steps. Charcoal and dialysis appear together among the treatment measures on slide 62 and act in different compartments, charcoal in the gut lumen and dialysis in the blood, so the choice depends on where the drug is.'},
  {t:'At any time, since charcoal binds drug in the bloodstream', correct:false,
   why:'Charcoal is not absorbed and stays in the gastrointestinal tract, which is the reason it is safe and also the reason it only works on drug that is still there. Removing drug already in the blood is what hemoperfusion and dialysis are for.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxDefense,
 teach:'Every treatment in this section is defined by the compartment it can reach, and the timing follows from that. Charcoal works in the gut lumen, so it is limited to the first hour in a simple ingestion and extends further only when recycling keeps returning drug there. Forced diuresis and ion trapping work in the renal tubule and need an ionizable drug. Dialysis, hemoperfusion, hemofiltration and exchange transfusion work on blood and suit drugs that stay in the circulation rather than distributing into tissue, which is why a highly lipophilic drug such as amiodarone is a poor candidate. Antidotes are the separate arm, acting on the chemistry rather than the location.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 62',
 quote:'PO activated charcoal (within 1h of toxin ingestion)'},

{id:'tx-30', prof:'Sikazwe', tier:'new', topic:'tox', sub:'def', concept:'enhanced-elimination-methods',
 tags:['recall'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which of these is a method of enhanced elimination in the treatment of toxicity?',
 options:[
  {t:'Forced diuresis and ion trapping', correct:true,
   why:'Both act on the kidney. Forced diuresis increases urine flow so less time is available for reabsorption, and ion trapping applies the pH trapping concept from slide 14 as therapy, keeping an ionizable drug charged so it cannot diffuse back.'},
  {t:'Enzyme induction with rifampin', correct:false,
   why:'Induction raises metabolic capacity over days, from MCMet-9_15_2026.pdf, which is far too slow for a poisoning and is not among the listed measures.'},
  {t:'Plasma protein supplementation', correct:false,
   why:'Raising binding would hold more drug in plasma and lower the free fraction available for filtration, so it would slow elimination rather than enhance it.'},
  {t:'Inhibiting renal transporters with probenecid', correct:false,
   why:'That is the deliberate opposite, slide 13, used to keep penicillin in the body longer. In a poisoning it is the wrong direction entirely.'}],
 svg:DIA.toxDefense,
 teach:'Enhanced elimination means speeding up a route the body already has, and the listed methods split by compartment. Forced diuresis and ion trapping act in the renal tubule and need a drug that is filtered and, for trapping, ionizable. Dialysis, hemoperfusion and hemofiltration act on blood and suit drugs with low volume of distribution and low protein binding, since only drug in the circulation can be removed. Exchange transfusion is the extreme version of the same idea. Choosing among them means asking where the drug actually is, which is why the physicochemical properties from earlier in the course decide the answer as much as the poisoning itself does.',
 cite:'Lecture #15 (MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf) slide 62',
 quote:'Enhanced elimination (forced diuresis, ion trapping, dialysis, hemoperfusion, hemofiltration, exchange transfusion, etc.)'}
);
